an be absent  for example  this word means i saw them the verb base  is itta o is a past tense marker it's  used in many varieties of nawat and in  some cases optionally but in orisaba  nawat it's required for all past tenses  preterite imperfective and past perfect  ni is a subject pronoun prefix meaning i  kim is a third person plural object  pronoun prefix in other words it means  them the k marks the third person while  im marks the plural  is a preterite marker in other words it  marks the simple past tense the plural  form is different and the preterit  marker differs depending on the class of  verbs  if the sentence contains explicit  subjects or objects or additional  information it can have more words  this means i saw your parents at your  home nay is a standalone subject pronoun  meaning i which is optional the subject  pronoun prefix ni is attached to the  verb so the standalone pronoun isn't  required  when there are standalone subject and  object pronouns or explicit subjects and  objects modern varieties of nawa tend to  have svo word order subject verb object  this is partly due to the influence of  spanish and is different from the sov  and vso word orders which were common in  classical nawat but modern varieties  don't always use svo and there's  flexibility in the word order  next moapas means your parents mo is the  second person singular possessive prefix  in other words your and apas means  parents and comes from spanish papas  then there's mochan which means your  home or your residence again mo is the  possessive prefix you can see the rest  of the possessive prefixes on the chart  here  as i mentioned before this contains the  preterit tense or simple past tense  suffix  this is suffixed to the verb's  perfective base or base two there are  three different verb bases that are used  depending on the tense though in some  cases they take the same form as each  other depending on the verb if we wanted  to use the imperfect tense we would need  to use the verb's imperfective base or  base one for this verb it's the same as  base two from a moment ago in orisaba  nawat the o prefix is used here as well  but a different suffix is used  yeah  this also means i saw them but is  imperfective meaning it was ongoing over  a period of time  without the prefix o and the suffix yah  this word would be in the present tense  i see them the verb base for present  tense is the same imperfective base base  one then there's the future tense the  future tense uses base three which is  used for future or hypothetical verb  forms but again in some cases it's the  same as one or both of the other bases  let's look at a different sentence in  the future tense nikala  this means i will enter his house or her  house again we see the subject pronoun  prefix and here is the verb base and  here the suffix  is the future tense suffix  means his house or her house kal means  house and e is the third person singular  possessive prefix  if you want to show possession with an  explicit possessor you keep the  possessive prefix but add the possessor  before it  meaning his house becomes quanical  meaning juan's house nicolas  i will enter juan's house  when the subject is third person  singular there's no subject pronoun  prefix  he wants to sleep literally he wants it  he will sleep the verb base meaning want  here is nikki so if there's no subject  prefix what's the k prefix here that's  the third person singular object prefix  this verb is transitive so it needs an  object prefix even though the object  comes right after it the first verb here  is in the present tense the second verb  is in the future tense again there's no  subject pronoun prefix  there's the verb base meaning to sleep  and the future suffix  in the english sentence there's an  infinitive form to sleep but nawat  doesn't have an infinitive form  imperatives the imperative prefix she is  used before the verb base  the verb base is qua so what's this  is an unspecified object prefix like  things or something this is a transitive  verb so it needs an object prefix of  some sort if there's no specific object  you can use an unspecified object prefix  like this of course there could be a  different object prefix if it's  referring to something specific  eat it this is the same prefix she with  a third person singular object pronoun  after it there's also an honorific  suffix that can be used for the  imperative forms  the plural imperative is made by adding  the suffix  note that y'all comes from you all and  is not used in standard english but is a  way of using the second person plural in  english  eat it or eat it y'all  object pronoun prefixes can also be  added to imperatives  help us or help us y'all  tech is an object pronoun prefix meaning  us  this imperative is negative  don't eat a lot of meat word for word  it's not much meat eat it plural  imperative form  there are two negation markers used in  urisaba  this is used in non-declarative  sentences in other words imperative or  subjunctive sentences or questions match  this is used in declarative sentences  like regular statements for example much  negan against  i don't want to eat or i don't want to  eat anything word for word it's not i  want it i will eat something the use of  these negation words differs from place  to place and in some places only amo is  used  adjectives most adjectives are derived  from nouns or verbs through affixation  though others are standalone adjectives  for example there's the noun  meaning stone this word consists of the  noun stem te plus the absolute suffix  then there's the adjective  meaning hard this word consists of te  plus the suffix teak teak is the most  common adjective suffix but there are  others as well  means salt and  means white  is another adjective suffix  is a verb meaning he died  means dead ki is a past tense suffix but  when used at the end of an adjective  it's like a past participle in other  words with a passive meaning  in a noun phrase the adjective usually  comes after the noun  a white stone but it can come before the  noun for extra emphasis  the plural forms of adjectives usually  have the suffix ke which replaces  white stones here we see that the plural  noun can take the plural suffix me which  is normally used for monosyllabic noun  stems and those ending in a vowel and  there's another one t  used for other noun stamps the absolute  suffix is removed and meh is added  another example  meaning woman becomes sibami meaning  women  there are some other less common plural  suffixes as well choco means teenager or  young man its plural form is  another case in which the absolute of  suffix is dropped is when one noun  connects to another one to form a  compound noun for example we have the  name of the language nawat there's also  this word which means the now what  language  it consists of the noun stem  with the absolute suffix removed plus  the word  which means language or word the li at  the end of the word is an absolute  suffix so in a compound noun like this  there can be just one absolute suffix at  the end after the final noun component  another similar example  this means an indigenous person le is  the absolute suffix we can remove it and  make a compound noun  this means indigenous language but can  be used to refer specifically to nawat  and we can remove the absolute suffix  from this word and make it part of a  compound noun  this means translator this word consists  of  meaning language or word plus quepa  meaning to turn or transfer plus ni  which means he who does in other words  the doer of an action  because of extensive contact between  nawat and spanish for several centuries  it's not surprising that there are lots  of spanish loanwords in nawat here are a  few basic examples cafe meaning coffee  meaning horse another word for horses  which means beast in spanish  meaning tissue from spanish panuello  this comes from senora and means miss or  lady but is often used in a  disrespectful way to talk about less  traditional women who live in the city  you may not be aware of it but there are  actually a number of words of nawat  origin that we commonly use in english  some of them are coyote from  avocado from  as you can see nawat is an incredibly  interesting language it's likely very  different from your native language but  it's also very logical and  straightforward the trouble with  learning it as far as i can see mainly  comes down to which variety to learn  since classical nawaz is not spoken  anymore and there are many varieties of  modern nawat which have no real  standardized form and even when you  choose a variety to learn you may have  trouble finding resources i've met  people who've learned nawaz to an  advanced level and they achieved that by  living in a nawat speaking part of  mexico and learning the local variety  the question of the day for speakers of  nawat how well are you able to  communicate with speakers of other  varieties of nawat what factors does it  depend on and to other people does nawat  have any features that are similar to  ones in your language what are they if  you like this video and you're  interested in mexico then check out my  video about mexico on my second channel  the geofocus channel click the link on  screen or in the video description and  it's time to say thank you to the  langfocus patreon supporters especially  the ones who appear on the screen they  are the top tier patreon supporters so  many extra special thanks to them check  out patreon.com langfocus to see how you  can become a patron  and to everyone thank you for watching  and have a nice day  [Music]  [Music]  you

